0 0 0 fC
225 227 1723 (The Ext field counts the number of physical extents allocated to the file. The Ext count) 1723 SB
225 286 1855 (corresponds to the number of directory entries given to the file. Depending on allocation size,) 1855 SB
225 345 1931 (there can be up to 128K bytes \(8 logical extents\) directly addressed by a single directory entry. In) 1931 SB
225 405 1886 (a special case, there are actually 256K bytes that can be directly addressed by a physical extent.) 1886 SB
225 523 1858 (The Acc field gives the R/O or R/W file indicator, which you can change using the commands) 1858 SB
225 582 674 (shown. The four command forms,) 674 SB
225 700 540 (STAT d:filename.typ $R/O) 540 SB
225 760 550 (STAT d:filename.typ $R/W) 550 SB
225 819 549 (STAT d:filename.typ $SYS) 549 SB
225 878 543 (STAT d:filename.typ $DIR) 543 SB
225 996 1932 (set or reset various permanent file indicators. The R/O indicator places the file, or set of files, in a) 1932 SB
225 1055 1901 (Read-Only status until changed by a subsequent STAT command. The R/O status is recorded in) 1901 SB
225 1115 1876 (the directory with the file so that it remains R/O through intervening cold start operations. The) 1876 SB
225 1174 1885 (R/W indicator places the file in a permanent Read-Write status. The SYS indicator attaches the) 1885 SB
225 1233 1729 (system indicator to the file, while the DIR command removes the system indicator. The) 1729 SB
225 1292 1922 (filename.typ may be ambiguous or unambiguous, but files whose attributes are changed are listed) 1922 SB
225 1351 1614 (at the console when the change occurs. The drive name denoted by d: is optional.) 1614 SB
225 92 498 (1.6 Transient Commands) 498 SB
1526 92 649 (CP/M Operating System Manual) 649 SB
