225 641 1829 (listed at the console using the TYPE command, or sent to a peripheral device using PIP \(see) 1829 SB
225 700 1795 (Section 1.6.4\). Note that the PRN file contains the original source program, augmented by) 1795 SB
225 760 1905 (miscellaneous assembly information in the leftmost 16 columns; for example, program addresses) 1905 SB
225 819 1938 (and hexadecimal machine code. The PRN file serves as a backup for the original source file. If the) 1938 SB
225 878 1823 (source file is accidentally removed or destroyed, the PRN file can be edited by removing the) 1823 SB
225 937 1893 (leftmost 16 characters of each line \(see Section 2\). This is done by issuing a single editor macro) 1893 SB
225 996 1910 (command. The resulting file is identical to the original source file and can be renamed from PRN) 1910 SB
225 1055 1065 (to ASM for subsequent editing and assembly. The file) 1065 SB
