225 582 1916 (The LOAD command reads the file ufn, which is assumed to contain HEX format machine code,) 1916 SB
225 641 1770 (and produces a memory image file that can subsequently be executed. The filename ufn is) 1770 SB
225 700 532 (assumed to be of the form:) 532 SB
225 819 152 (X.HEX) 152 SB
225 937 1872 (and only the filename X need be specified in the command. The LOAD command creates a file) 1872 SB
225 996 130 (named) 130 SB
225 1115 162 (X.COM) 162 SB
225 1233 1910 (that marks it as containing machine executable code. The file is actually loaded into memory and) 1910 SB
225 1292 1924 (executed when the user types the filename X immediately after the prompting character > printed) 1924 SB
225 1351 241 (by the CCP.) 241 SB
225 1470 1805 (Generally, the CCP reads the filename X following the prompting character and looks for a) 1805 SB
225 1529 1904 (built-in function name. If no function name is found, the CCP searches the system disk directory) 1904 SB
225 1588 419 (for a file by the name) 419 SB
225 1706 162 (X.COM) 162 SB
225 1825 1942 (If found, the machine code is loaded into the TPA, and the program executes. Thus, the user need) 1942 SB
225 1884 1872 (only LOAD a hex file once; it can be subsequently executed any number of times by typing the) 1872 SB
225 1943 1908 (primary name. This way, you can invent new commands in the CCP. Initialized disks contain the) 1908 SB
225 2002 1911 (transient commands as COM files, which are optionally deleted. The operation takes place on an) 1911 SB
225 2061 1260 (alternate drive if the filename is prefixed by a drive name. Thus,) 1260 SB
225 2180 327 (LOAD B:BETA) 327 SB
225 2298 1899 (brings the LOAD program into the TPA from the currently logged disk and operates on drive B) 1899 SB
225 2357 448 (after execution begins.) 448 SB
