225 879 73 (PIP) 73 SB
225 938 945 (PlP destination=source#1,source#2,...,source#n) 945 SB
225 1056 1842 (PIP is the CP/M Peripheral Interchange Program that implements the basic media conversion) 1842 SB
225 1116 1829 (operations necessary to load, print, punch, copy, and combine disk files. The PIP program is) 1829 SB
225 1175 911 (initiated by typing one of the following forms:) 911 SB
225 1293 73 (PIP) 73 SB
225 1352 362 (PIP command line) 362 SB
225 1471 1945 (In both cases PIP is loaded into the TPA and executed. In the first form, PIP reads command lines) 1945 SB
225 1530 1884 (directly from the console, prompted with the * character, until an empty command line is typed) 1884 SB
225 1589 1878 (\(for example, a single carriage return is issued by the operator\). Each successive command line) 1878 SB
225 1648 1605 (causes some media conversion to take place according to the rules shown below.) 1605 SB
225 1766 1884 (In the second form, the PIP command is equivalent to the first, except that the single command) 1884 SB
225 1826 1921 (line given with the PIP command is automatically executed, and PIP terminates immediately with) 1921 SB
225 1885 1903 (no further prompting of the console for input command lines. The form of each command line is) 1903 SB
225 2003 864 (destination=source#1,source#2,...,source#n) 864 SB
225 2121 1943 (where destination is the file or peripheral device to receive the data, and source#1,...,source#n is a) 1943 SB
225 2181 1752 (series of one or more files or devices that are copied from left to right to the destination.) 1752 SB
