225 227 1833 (When multiple files are given in the command line \(for example, n>1\), the individual files are) 1833 SB
225 286 1912 (assumed to contain ASCII characters, with an assumed CP/M end-of-file character \(CTRL-Z\) at) 1912 SB
225 345 1775 (the end of each file \(see the O parameter to override this assumption\). Lower-case ASCII) 1775 SB
225 405 1950 (alphabetics are internally translated to upper-case to be consistent with CP/M file and device name) 1950 SB
225 464 1950 (conventions. Finally, the total command line length cannot exceed 255 characters. CTRL-E can be) 1950 SB
225 523 1560 (used to force a physical carriage return for lines that exceed the console width.) 1560 SB
225 641 1865 (The destination and source elements are unambiguous references to CP/M source files with or) 1865 SB
225 700 1867 (without a preceding disk drive name. That is, any file can be referenced with a preceding drive) 1867 SB
225 760 1866 (name \(A: through P:\) that defines the particular drive where the file can be obtained or stored.) 1866 SB
225 819 1874 (When the drive name is not included, the currently logged disk is assumed. The destination file) 1874 SB
225 878 1935 (can also appear as one or more of the source files; in which case the source file is not altered until) 1935 SB
225 937 1825 (the entire concatenation is complete. If it already exists, the destination file is removed if the) 1825 SB
225 996 1859 (command line is properly formed. It is not removed if an error condition arises. The following) 1859 SB
225 1055 1419 (command lines, with explanations to the right, are valid as input to PIP:) 1419 SB
225 1174 139 (   X=Y) 139 SB
825 1174 1238 (Copies to file X from file Y, where X and Y are unambiguous ) 1238 SB
825 1233 652 (filenames; Y remains unchanged.) 652 SB
225 1351 182 (   X=Y,Z) 182 SB
825 1351 1237 (Concatenates files Y and Z and copies to file X, with Y and Z ) 1237 SB
825 1410 226 (unchanged.) 226 SB
225 1529 542 (   X.ASM=Y.ASM,Z.ASM) 542 SB
825 1529 1212 (Creates the file X.ASM from the concatenation of the Y and ) 1212 SB
825 1588 256 (Z.ASM files.) 256 SB
225 1706 545 (   NEW.ZOT=B:OLD.ZAP) 545 SB
825 1706 1300 (Moves a copy of OLD.ZAP from drive B to the currently logged ) 1300 SB
825 1765 628 (disk; names the file NEW.ZOT.) 628 SB
225 1884 579 (   B:A.U=B:B.V,A:C.W,D.X) 579 SB
825 1884 1312 (   Concatenates file B.Y from drive B with C.W from drive A and ) 1312 SB
825 1943 1154 (D.X from the logged disk; creates the file A.U on drive B.) 1154 SB
