225 227 1877 (PIP allows reference to physical and logical devices that are attached to the CP/M svstem. The) 1877 SB
225 286 1911 (device names are the same as given under the STAT command, along with a number of specially) 1911 SB
225 345 1687 (named devices. The following is a list of logical devices given in the STAT command) 1687 SB
375 464 317 (CON: \(console\)) 317 SB
375 523 288 (RDR: \(reader\)) 288 SB
375 582 281 (PUN: \(punch\)) 281 SB
375 641 208 (LST: \(list\)) 208 SB
225 760 585 (while the physical devices are) 585 SB
375 878 760 (TTY: \(console , reader, punch, or list\)) 760 SB
375 937 781 (CRT: \(console, or list\), UC1: \(console\)) 781 SB
375 996 882 (PTR: \(reader\), URI: \(reader\), UR2: \(reader\)) 882 SB
375 1055 852 (PTP: \(punch\), UPI: \(punch\), UP2: \(punch\)) 852 SB
375 1115 436 (LPT: \(list\), ULI: \(list\)) 436 SB
225 1233 1944 (The BAT: physical device is not included, because this assignment is used only to indicate that the) 1944 SB
225 1292 1160 (RDR: and LST: devices are used for console input/output.) 1160 SB
225 1410 1950 (The RDR, LST, PUN, and CON devices are all defined within the BIOS portion of CP/M, and are) 1950 SB
225 1470 1856 (easily altered for any particular I/O system. The current physical device mapping is defined by) 1856 SB
225 1529 1910 (IOBYTE; see Section 6 for a discussion of this function. The destination device must be capable) 1910 SB
225 1588 1903 (of receiving data, for example, data cannot be sent to the punch, and the source devices must be) 1903 SB
225 1647 1442 (capable of generating data, for example, the LST: device cannot be read.) 1442 SB
