225 760 1930 (File and device names can be interspersed in the PIP commands.  In each case, the specific device) 1930 SB
225 819 1932 (is read until end-of-file \(CTRL-Z for ASCII files, and end-of-data for non-ASCII disk files\). Data) 1932 SB
225 878 1841 (from each device or file are concatenated from left to right until the last data source has been) 1841 SB
225 937 99 (read.) 99 SB
225 1055 1858 (The destination device or file is written using the data from the source files, and an end-of-file) 1858 SB
225 1115 1857 (character, CTRL-Z, is appended to the result for ASCII files. If the destination is a disk file, a) 1857 SB
225 1174 1842 (temporary file is created \($$$ secondary name\) that is changed to the actual filename only on) 1842 SB
225 1233 1806 (successful completion of the copy. Files with the extension COM are always assumed to be) 1806 SB
225 1292 234 (non-ASCII.) 234 SB
225 1410 1947 (The copy operation can be aborted at any time by pressing any key on the keyboard. PIP responds) 1947 SB
225 1470 1794 (with the message ABORTED to indicate that the operation has not been completed. If any) 1794 SB
225 1529 1942 (operation is aborted, or if an error occurs during processing, PIP removes any pending commands) 1942 SB
225 1588 1050 (that were set up while using the SUBMIT command.) 1050 SB
225 1706 1705 (PIP performs a special function if the destination is a disk file with type HEX \(an Intel) 1705 SB
225 1765 1925 (hex-formatted machine code file\), and the source is an external peripheral device, such as a paper) 1925 SB
225 1825 1941 (tape reader. In this case, the PIP program checks to ensure that the source file contains a properly) 1941 SB
225 1884 1379 (formed hex file, with legal hexadecimal values and checksum records.) 1379 SB
225 2002 1910 (When an invalid input record is found, PIP reports an error message at the console and waits for) 1910 SB
225 2061 1914 (corrective action.  Usually, you can open the reader and rerun a section of the tape \(pull the tape) 1914 SB
225 2120 1893 (back about 20 inches\). When the tape is ready for the reread, a single carriage return is typed at) 1893 SB
225 2180 1934 (the console, and PIP attempts another read. If the tape position cannot be properly read, continue) 1934 SB
225 2239 1881 (the read by typing a return following the error message, and enter the record manually with the) 1881 SB
225 2298 897 (ED program after the disk file is constructed.) 897 SB
225 2416 1897 (PIP allows the end-of-file to be entered from the console if the source file is an RDR: device. In) 1897 SB
225 2475 1935 (this case, the PIP program reads the device and monitors the keyboard. If CTRL-Z is typed at the) 1935 SB
225 2535 1039 (keyboard, the read operation is terminated normally.) 1039 SB
