375 464 144 (ED ufn) 144 SB
225 582 1911 (The ED program is the CP/M system context editor that allows creation and alteration of ASCII) 1911 SB
225 641 1898 (files in the CP/M environment.  Complete details of operation are given in Section 2. ED allows) 1898 SB
225 700 1878 (the operator to create and operate upon source files that are organized as a sequence of ASCII) 1878 SB
225 760 1936 (characters, separated by end-of-line characters \(a carriage return/line-feed sequence\).  There is no) 1936 SB
225 819 1934 (practical restriction on line length \(no single line can exceed the size of the working memory\) that) 1934 SB
225 878 1405 (is defined by the number of characters typed between carriage returns. ) 1405 SB
225 996 1848 (The ED program has a number of commands for character string searching, replacement, and) 1848 SB
225 1055 1929 (insertion that are useful for creating and correcting programs or text files under CP/M.  Although) 1929 SB
225 1115 1914 (the CP/M has a limited memory work space area \(approximately 5000 characters in'a 20K CP/M) 1914 SB
225 1174 1837 (system\), the file size that can be edited is not limited, since data are easily paged through this) 1837 SB
225 1233 213 (work area.) 213 SB
225 1351 1931 (If it does not exist, ED creates the specified source file and opens the file for access. If the source) 1931 SB
225 1410 1948 (file does exist, the programmer appends data for editing \(see the A command\). The appended data) 1948 SB
225 1470 1784 (can then be displayed, altered, and written from the work area back to the disk \(see the W) 1784 SB
225 1529 1892 (command\). Particular points in the program can be automatically paged and located by context,) 1892 SB
225 1588 1566 (allowing easy access to particular portions of a large file \(see the N command\).) 1566 SB
225 1706 795 (If you type the following command line:) 795 SB
225 1825 236 (ED X.ASM) 236 SB
225 1943 1288 (the ED program creates an intermediate work file with the name ) 1288 SB
225 2061 124 (X.$$$) 124 SB
225 2180 1926 (to hold the edited data during the ED run. Upon completion of ED, the X.ASM file \(original file\)) 1926 SB
225 2239 1828 (is renamed to X.BAK, and the edited work file is renamed to X.ASM. Thus, the X.BAK file) 1828 SB
225 2298 1947 (contains the original unedited file, and the X.ASM file contains the newly edited file. The operator) 1947 SB
225 2357 1799 (can always return to the previous version of a file by removing the most recent version and) 1799 SB
225 2416 1760 (renaming the previous version. If the current X.ASM file has been improperly edited, the) 1760 SB
225 2475 1135 (following sequence of commands reclaim the backup file.) 1135 SB
225 92 498 (1.6 Transient Commands) 498 SB
