225 227 1924 (The last command in a SUB file can initiate another SUB file, allowing chained batch commands.) 1924 SB
225 345 1673 (Suppose the file ASMBL.SUB exists on disk and contains the prototype commands:) 1673 SB
225 464 170 (ASM $1) 170 SB
225 523 187 (DIR $1.*) 187 SB
225 582 253 (ERA *.BAK) 253 SB
225 641 338 (PIP $2:=$1.PRN) 338 SB
225 700 272 (ERA $1.PRN) 272 SB
225 819 790 (then, you issue the following command:) 790 SB
225 937 531 (SUBMIT ASMBL X PRN) 531 SB
225 1055 1907 (The SUBMIT program reads the ASMBL.SUB file, substituting X for all occurrences of $1 and) 1907 SB
225 1115 1750 (PRN for all occurrences of $2.  This results in a $$$.SUB file containing the commands:) 1750 SB
225 1233 156 (ASM X) 156 SB
225 1292 173 (DIR X.*) 173 SB
