225 227 1942 (ED also provides a single command that combines the F and I commands to perform simple string) 1942 SB
225 286 1072 (substitutions. The command takes the following form:) 1072 SB
375 405 288 (nSs1^Zs2<cr>) 288 SB
375 523 43 (or) 43 SB
375 641 246 (nSs1^Zs2^Z) 246 SB
225 760 1815 (and has exactly the same effect as applying the following command string a total of n times:) 1815 SB
375 878 358 (Fs1^Z-kDIs2<cr>) 358 SB
375 996 43 (or) 43 SB
375 1115 316 (Fs1^Z-kDIs2^Z) 316 SB
225 1233 1931 (where k is the length of the string. ED searches the memory buffer starting at the current position) 1931 SB
225 1292 1912 (of CP and successively substitutes the second string for the first string untill the end of buffer, or) 1912 SB
225 1351 976 (until the substitution has been performed n times.) 976 SB
225 1470 1837 (As a convenience, a command similar to F is provided by ED that automatically appends and) 1837 SB
225 1529 949 (writes lines as the search proceeds.  The form is) 949 SB
225 1647 174 (nNs<cr>) 174 SB
225 1765 43 (or) 43 SB
225 1884 132 (nNs^Z) 132 SB
225 2002 1937 (which searches the entire source file for the nth occurrence of the strings \(you should recall that F) 1937 SB
225 2061 1810 (fails if the string cannot be found in the current buffer\). The operation of the N command is) 1810 SB
225 2120 1805 (precisely the same as F except in the case that the string cannot be found within the current) 1805 SB
225 2180 1846 (memory buffer. In this case, the entire memory content is written \(that is, an automatic #W is) 1846 SB
225 2239 1840 (issued\). Input lines are then read until the buffer is at least half full, or the entire source file is) 1840 SB
225 2298 1943 (exhausted. The search continues in this manner until the string has been found n times, or until the) 1943 SB
225 2357 1290 (source file has been completely transferred to the temporary file. ) 1290 SB
