225 1647 1926 (Q is an alternate radix indicator for octal numbers because the letter O is easily confused with the) 1926 SB
225 1706 1931 (digit 0. Any numeric constant that does not terminate with a radix indicator is a decimal constant.) 1931 SB
225 1825 1948 (A constant is composed as a sequence of digits, followed by an optional radix indicator, where the) 1948 SB
225 1884 1880 (digits are in the appropriate range for the radix. Binary constants must be composed of 0 and 1) 1880 SB
225 1943 1935 (digits, octal constants can contain digits in the range 0-7, while decimal constants contain decimal) 1935 SB
225 2002 1795 (digits. Hexadecimal constants contain decimal digits as well as hexadecimal digits A\(10D\),) 1795 SB
225 2061 1839 (B\(11D\), C\(12D\), D\(13D\), E\(14D\), and F\(15D\). Note that the leading digit of a hexadecimal) 1839 SB
225 2120 1899 (constant must be a decimal digit to avoid confusing a hexadecimal constant with an identifier. A) 1899 SB
225 2180 1825 (leading 0 will always suffice.  A constant composed in this manner must evaluate to a binary) 1825 SB
225 2239 1929 (number that can be contained within a 16-bit counter, otherwise it is truncated on the right by the) 1929 SB
225 2298 207 (assembler.) 207 SB
