225 345 1921 (As a convenience to the programmer, ASM assumes that operators have a relative precedence of) 1921 SB
225 405 1930 (application that allows the programmer to write expressions without nested levels of parentheses.) 1930 SB
225 464 1941 (The resulting expression has assumed parentheses that are defined by the relative precedence. The) 1941 SB
225 523 1909 (order of application of operators in unparenthesized expressions is listed below. Operators listed) 1909 SB
225 582 1821 (first have highest precedence \(they are applied first in an unparenthesized expression\), while) 1821 SB
225 641 1772 (operators listed last have lowest precedence. Operators listed on the same line have equal) 1772 SB
225 700 1734 (precedence, and are applied from left to right as they are encountered in an expression. ) 1734 SB
225 819 398 (* / MOD SHL SHR) 398 SB
225 878 58 (- +) 58 SB
225 937 103 (NOT) 103 SB
225 996 107 (AND) 107 SB
225 1055 187 (OR XOR) 187 SB
