225 227 530 (3.4.1 The ORG Directive) 530 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c322 font
225 345 711 (The ORG statement takes the form:) 711 SB
375 464 433 (label ORG expression) 433 SB
225 582 1893 (where label is an optional program identifier and expression is a 16-bit expression, consisting of) 1893 SB
225 641 1769 (operands that are defined before the ORG statement. The assembler begins machine code) 1769 SB
225 700 1736 (generation at the location specified in the expression. There can be any number of ORG) 1736 SB
225 760 1920 (statements within a particular program, and there are no checks to ensure that the programmer is) 1920 SB
225 819 1878 (not defining overlapping memory areas. Note that most programs written for the CP/M system) 1878 SB
225 878 838 (begin with an ORG statement of the form:) 838 SB
225 996 229 (ORG 100H) 229 SB
225 1115 1943 (which causes machine code generation to begin at the base of the CP/M transient program area. If) 1943 SB
225 1174 1919 (a label is specified in the ORG statement, the label is given the value of the expression. This label) 1919 SB
225 1233 1688 (can then be used in the operand field of other statements to represent this expression.) 1688 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 46 /MSTT31c356 font
225 1351 521 (3.4.2 The END Directive) 521 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c322 font
225 1470 1944 (The END statement is optional in an assembly-language program, but if it is present it must be the) 1944 SB
225 1529 1904 (last statement. All subsequent statements are ignored in the assembly. The END statement takes) 1904 SB
225 1588 493 (the following two forms:) 493 SB
225 1706 208 (label END) 208 SB
225 1825 431 (label END expression) 431 SB
225 1943 1845 (where the label is again optional. If the first form is used, the assembly process stops, and the) 1845 SB
225 2002 1900 (default starting address of the program is taken as 0000. Otherwise, the expression is evaluated,) 1900 SB
225 2061 1896 (and becomes the program starting address. This starting address is included in the last record of) 1896 SB
225 2120 1800 (the Intel-formatted machine code hex file that results from the assembly. Thus, most CP/M) 1800 SB
225 2180 1049 (assembly-language programs end with the statement:) 1049 SB
225 2298 227 (END 100H) 227 SB
225 2416 1786 (resulting in the default starting address of 100H \(beginning of the transient program area\).) 1786 SB
225 92 497 (3.4 Assembler Directives) 497 SB
1526 92 649 (CP/M Operating System Manual) 649 SB
1154 2942 92 (3-11) 92 SB
