225 464 348 (4.1 Introduction) 348 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c322 font
225 582 1947 (The DDT program allows dynamic interactive testing and debugging of programs generated in the) 1947 SB
225 641 1765 (CP/M environment. Invoke the debugger with a command of one of the following forms:) 1765 SB
375 760 103 (DDT) 103 SB
375 819 398 (DDT filename.HEX) 398 SB
375 878 408 (DDT filename.COM) 408 SB
225 996 1808 (where filename is the name of the program to be loaded and tested. In both cases, the DDT) 1808 SB
225 1055 1874 (program is brought into main memory in place of the Console Command Processor \(CCP\) and) 1874 SB
225 1115 1811 (resides directly below the Basic Disk Operating System \(BDOS\) portion of CP/M. Refer to) 1811 SB
225 1174 1903 (Section 5 for standard memory organization. The BDOS starting address, located in the address) 1903 SB
225 1233 1864 (field of the JMP instruction at location 5H, is altered to reflect the reduced Transient Program) 1864 SB
225 1292 351 (Area \(TPA\) size. ) 351 SB
225 1410 1882 (The second and third forms of the DDT command perform the same actions as the first, except) 1882 SB
225 1470 1920 (there is a subsequent automatic load of the specified HEX or COM file. The action is identical to) 1920 SB
225 1529 749 (the following sequence of commands:) 749 SB
375 1647 103 (DDT) 103 SB
375 1706 677 (Ifilename.HEX or Ifilename.COM) 677 SB
375 1765 33 (R) 33 SB
225 1884 1943 (where the I and R commands set up and read the specified program to test. See the explanation of) 1943 SB
225 1943 921 (the I and R commands below for exact details.) 921 SB
225 2061 1171 (Upon initiation, DDT prints a sign-on message in the form:) 1171 SB
375 2180 316 (DDT VER m.m) 316 SB
225 2298 683 (where m.m is the revision number.) 683 SB
1166 2942 67 (4-1) 67 SB
