225 819 816 (     U         untraced program monitoring.) 816 SB
225 937 1117 (     X         examines and optionally alters the CPU state.) 1117 SB
225 1055 1819 (The command character, in some cases, is followed by zero, one, two, or three hexadecimal) 1819 SB
225 1115 1908 (values, which are separated by commas or single blank characters. All DDT numeric output is in) 1908 SB
225 1174 1931 (hexadecimal form. The commands are not executed until the carriage return is typed at the end of) 1931 SB
225 1233 280 (the command.) 280 SB
225 1351 1895 (At any point in the debug run, you can stop execution of DDT by using either a CTRL-C or G0) 1895 SB
225 1410 1917 (\(jump to location 0000H\) and save the current memory image by using a SAVE command of the) 1917 SB
225 1470 109 (form:) 109 SB
375 1588 485 (SAVE n filename. COM) 485 SB
225 1706 1877 (where n is the number of pages \(256 byte blocks\) to be saved on disk. The number of blocks is) 1877 SB
225 1765 1937 (determined by taking the high-order byte of the address in the TPA and converting this number to) 1937 SB
225 1825 1894 (decimal. For example, if the highest address in the TPA is 134H, the number of pages is 12H or) 1894 SB
225 1884 1811 (18 in decimal. You could type a CTRL-C during the debug run, returning to the CCP level,) 1811 SB
225 1943 234 (followed by) 234 SB
225 2061 381 (SAVE 18 X. COM) 381 SB
225 2180 1858 (The memory image is saved as X.COM on the disk and can be directly executed by typing the) 1858 SB
225 2239 1637 (name X. If further testing is required, the memory image can be recalled by typing ) 1637 SB
225 2357 278 (DDT X.COM) 278 SB
225 2475 1885 (which reloads the previously saved program from location 100H through page 18, 23FFH. The) 1885 SB
225 2535 1900 (CPU state is not a part of the COM file; thus, the program must be restarted from the beginning) 1900 SB
225 2594 358 (to test it properly.) 358 SB
225 92 324 (4.1 Introduction) 324 SB
