225 227 437 (4.2 DDT Commands) 437 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c322 font
225 345 1928 (The individual commands are detailed below. In each case, the operator must wait for the hyphen) 1928 SB
225 405 1914 (prompt character before entering the command. If control is passed to a program under test, and) 1914 SB
225 464 1926 (the program has not reached a breakpoint, control can be returned to DDT by executing a RST 7) 1926 SB
225 523 1916 (from the front panel.  In the explanation of each command, the command letter is shown in some) 1916 SB
225 582 1844 (cases with numbers separated by commas, the numbers are represented by lower-case letters.) 1844 SB
225 641 1866 (These numbers are always assumed to be in a hexadecimal radix and from one to four digits in) 1866 SB
225 700 1281 (length. Longer numbers are automatically truncated on the right.) 1281 SB
225 819 1869 (Many of the commands operate upon a CPU state that corresponds to the program under test.) 1869 SB
225 878 1911 (The CPU state holds the registers of the program being debugged and initially contains zeros for) 1911 SB
225 937 1873 (all registers and flags except for the program counter, P, and stack pointer, S, which default to) 1873 SB
225 996 1948 (100H. The program counter is subsequently set to the starting address given in the last record of a) 1948 SB
225 1055 1321 (HEX file if a file of this form is loaded, see the I and R commands.) 1321 SB
