0 0 0 fC
225 227 1859 (The second form of the T command is similar to the first, except that execution is traced for n) 1859 SB
225 286 1916 (steps \(n is a hexadecimal value\) before a program breakpoint occurs. A breakpoint can be forced) 1916 SB
225 345 1902 (in the trace mode by typing a rubout character. The CPU state is displayed before each program) 1902 SB
225 405 1933 (step is taken in trace mode. The format of the display is the same as described in the X command.) 1933 SB
225 523 1862 (You should note that program tracing is discontinued at the CP/M interface and resumes after) 1862 SB
225 582 1928 (return from CP/M to the program under test. Thus, CP/M functions that access I/O devices, such) 1928 SB
225 641 1925 (as the disk drive, run in real-time, avoiding I/O timing problems. Programs running in trace mode) 1925 SB
225 700 1922 (execute approximately 500 times slower than real-time because DDT gets control after each user) 1922 SB
225 760 1878 (instruction is executed. Interrupt processing routines can be traced, but commands that use the) 1878 SB
225 819 1900 (breakpoint facility \(G, T, and U\) accomplish the break using an RST 7 instruction, which means) 1900 SB
225 878 1941 (that the tested program cannot use this interrupt location. Further, the trace mode always runs the) 1941 SB
225 937 1927 (tested program with interrupts enabled, which may cause problems if asynchronous interrupts are) 1927 SB
225 996 470 (received during tracing.) 470 SB
225 1115 1902 (To get control back to DDT during trace, press RETURN rather than executing an RST 7. This) 1902 SB
225 1174 1534 (ensures that the trace for current instruction is completed before interruption.) 1534 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 46 /MSTT31c356 font
225 1292 726 (4.2.11 The U \(Untrace\) Command) 726 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c322 font
225 1410 1883 (The U command is identical to the T command, except that intermediate program steps are not) 1883 SB
225 1470 1947 (displayed. The untrace mode allows from 1 to 65535 \(0FFFFH\) steps to be executed in monitored) 1947 SB
225 1529 1853 (mode and is used principally to retain control of an executing program while it reaches steady) 1853 SB
225 1588 1508 (state conditions. All conditions of the T command apply to the U command.) 1508 SB
