225 227 1890 (In particular, the BDOS address at location 6H \(address field of the JMP instruction at location) 1890 SB
225 286 1943 (5H\) is modified by DDT to address the base location of the DDT nucleus, which, in turn, contains) 1943 SB
225 345 1942 (a JMP instruction to the BDOS. Thus, programs that use this address field to size memory see the) 1942 SB
225 405 1763 (logical end of memory at the base of the DDT nucleus rather than the base of the BDOS.) 1763 SB
225 523 1809 (The assembler/disassembler module resides directly below the DDT nucleus in the transient) 1809 SB
225 582 1880 (program area. If the A, L, T, or X commands are used during the debugging process, the DDT) 1880 SB
225 641 1882 (program again alters the address field at 6H to include this module, further reducing the logical) 1882 SB
225 700 1900 (end of memory. If a program loads beyond the beginning of the assembler/disassembler module,) 1900 SB
225 760 1900 (the A and L commands are lost \(their use produces a ? in response\) and the trace and display \(T) 1900 SB
225 819 1784 (and X\) commands list the inst field of the display in hexadecimal, rather than as a decoded) 1784 SB
