225 464 348 (5.1 Introduction) 348 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c322 font
225 582 1889 (This chapter describes CP/M \(release 2\) system organization including the structure of memory) 1889 SB
225 641 1884 (and system entry points. This section provides the information you need to write programs that) 1884 SB
225 700 1667 (operate under CP/M and that use the peripheral and disk I/O facilities of the system.) 1667 SB
225 819 1933 (CP/M is logically divided into four parts, called the Basic Input/Output System \(BIOS\), the Basic) 1933 SB
225 878 1839 (Disk Operating System \(BDOS\), the Console Command Processor \(CCP\), and the Transient) 1839 SB
225 937 1935 (Program Area \(TPA\). The BIOS is a hardware-dependent module that defines the exact low level) 1935 SB
225 996 1921 (interface with a particular computer system that is necessary for peripheral device I/O.  Although) 1921 SB
225 1055 1800 (a standard BIOS is supplied by Digital Research, explicit instructions are provided for field) 1800 SB
225 1115 1721 (reconfiguration of the BIOS to match nearly any hardware environment, see Section 6.) 1721 SB
225 1233 1937 (The BIOS and BDOS are logically combined into a single module with a common entry point and) 1937 SB
225 1292 1761 (referred to as the FDOS. The CCP is a distinct program that uses the FDOS to provide a) 1761 SB
225 1351 1872 (human-oriented interface with the information that is cataloged on the back-up storage device.) 1872 SB
225 1410 1820 (The TPA is an area of memory, not used by the FDOS and CCP, where various nonresident) 1820 SB
225 1470 1866 (operating system commands and user programs are executed. The lower portion of memory is) 1866 SB
225 1529 1831 (reserved for system information and is detailed in later sections. Memory organization of the) 1831 SB
225 1588 736 (CP/M system is shown in Figure 5-1.) 736 SB
1166 2942 67 (5-1) 67 SB
