791 1410 818 (Figure 5-1.  CP/M Memory Organization) 818 SB
225 1529 1914 (The exact memory addresses corresponding to BOOT, TBASE, CBASE, and FBASE vary from) 1914 SB
225 1588 1783 (version to version and are described fully n Section 6. All standard CP/M versions assume) 1783 SB
225 1647 1931 (BOOT=0000H, which is the base of random access memory. The machine code found at location) 1931 SB
225 1706 1824 (BOOT performs a system warm start, which loads and initializes the programs and variables) 1824 SB
225 1765 1831 (necessary to return control to the CCP. Thus, transient programs need only jump to location) 1831 SB
225 1825 1875 (BOOT to return control to CP/M at the command level.  further, the standard versions assume) 1875 SB
225 1884 1825 (TBASE=BOOT+0100H, which is normally location 0100H. The principal entry point to the) 1825 SB
225 1943 1825 (FDOS is at location BOOT+0005H \(normally 0005H\) where a jump to BASE is found. The) 1825 SB
225 2002 1927 (address field at BOOT+0006H \(normally 006H\) contains the value of FBASE and can be used to) 1927 SB
225 2061 1866 (determine the size of available memory, assuming that the CCP is being overlaid by a transient) 1866 SB
225 2120 182 (program.) 182 SB
225 2180 13 ( ) 13 SB
