0 0 25 25 798 1100 300 SM
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c322 font
0 0 0 fC
225 227 1946 (Transient programs are loaded into the TPA and executed as follows. The operator communicates) 1946 SB
225 286 1904 (with the CCP by typing command lines following each prompt. Each command line takes one of) 1904 SB
225 345 403 (the following forms:) 403 SB
375 464 193 (command) 193 SB
375 523 292 (command file1) 292 SB
375 582 391 (command file1 file2) 391 SB
225 700 1858 (where command is either a built-in function, such as DIR or TYPE, or the name of a transient) 1858 SB
225 760 1916 (command or program. If the command is a built-in function of CP/M, it is executed immediately.) 1916 SB
225 819 1576 (Otherwise, the CCP searches the currently addressed disk for a file by the name) 1576 SB
225 937 319 (command.COM) 319 SB
225 1055 1943 (If the file is found, it is assumed to be a memory image of a program that executes in the TPA and) 1943 SB
225 1115 1885 (thus implicity originates at TBASE in memory. The CCP loads the COM file from the disk into) 1885 SB
225 1174 1158 (memory starting at TBASE and can extend up to CBASE.) 1158 SB
225 1292 1881 (If the command is followed by one or two file specifications, the CCP prepares one or two File) 1881 SB
225 1351 1889 (Control Block \(FCB\) names in the system parameter area. These optional FCBs are in the form) 1889 SB
225 1410 1518 (necessary to access files through the FDOS and are described in Section 5.2.) 1518 SB
225 1529 1948 (The transient program receives control from the CCP and begins execution, using the I/O facilities) 1948 SB
225 1588 1845 (of the FDOS. The transient program is called from the CCP. Thus, it can simply return to the) 1845 SB
225 1647 1916 (CCP upon completion of its processing, or can Jump to BOOT to pass control back to CP/M. In) 1916 SB
225 1706 1929 (the first case, the transient program must not use memory above CBASE, while in the latter case,) 1929 SB
225 1765 873 (memory up through FBASE-1 can be used. ) 873 SB
225 1884 1943 (The transient program can use the CP/M I/O facilities to communicate with the operator's console) 1943 SB
225 1943 1847 (and peripheral devices, including the disk subsystem. The I/O system is accessed by passing a) 1847 SB
225 2002 1721 (function number and an information address to CP/M through the FDOS entry point at) 1721 SB
225 2061 1892 (BOOT+0005H. In the case of a disk read, for example, the transient program sends the number) 1892 SB
225 2120 1912 (corresponding to a disk read, along with the address of an FCB to the CP/M FDOS. The FDOS,) 1912 SB
225 2180 1855 (in turn, performs the operation and returns with either a disk read completion indication or an) 1855 SB
225 2239 1188 (error number indicating that the disk read was unsuccessful.) 1188 SB
225 92 324 (5.1 Introduction) 324 SB
