225 1233 1876 (Source files are treated as a sequence of ASCII characters, where each line of the source file is) 1876 SB
225 1292 1798 (followed by a carriage return, and line-feed sequence \(0DH followed by 0AH\). Thus, one  ) 1798 SB
225 1351 1803 (128-byte CP/M record can contain several lines of source text.  The end of an ASCII file is) 1803 SB
225 1410 1937 (denoted by a CTRL-Z character \(1AH\) or a real end-of-file returned by the CP/M read operation.) 1937 SB
225 1470 1869 (CTRL-Z characters embedded within machine code files \(for example, COM files\) are ignored) 1869 SB
225 1529 1670 (and the end-of-file condition returned by CP/M is used to terminate read operations.) 1670 SB
