225 227 1761 (Files in CP/M can be thought of as a sequence of up to 65536 records of 128 bytes each,) 1761 SB
225 286 1785 (numbered from 0 through 65535, thus allowing a maximum of 8 megabytes per file. Note,) 1785 SB
225 345 1828 (however, that although the records may be considered logically contiguous, they may not be) 1828 SB
225 405 1941 (physically contiguous in the disk data area.  Internally, all files are divided into 16K byte segments) 1941 SB
225 464 1838 (called logical extents, so that counters are easily maintained as 8-bit values. The division into) 1838 SB
225 523 1881 (extents is discussed in the paragraphs that follow: however, they are not particularly significant) 1881 SB
225 582 1928 (for the programmer, because each extent is automatically accessed in both sequential and random) 1928 SB
225 641 281 (access modes.) 281 SB
225 760 1924 (In the file operations starting with Function 15, DE usually addresses a FCB. Transient programs) 1924 SB
225 819 1899 (often use the default FCB area reserved by CP/M at location BOOT+005CH \(normally 005CH\)) 1899 SB
225 878 1886 (for simple file operations. The basic unit of file information is a 128-byte record used for all file) 1886 SB
225 937 1907 (operations. Thus, a default location for disk I/O is provided by CP/M at location BOOT+0080H) 1907 SB
225 996 1528 (\(normally 0080H\) which is the initial default DMA address. See Function 26.) 1528 SB
225 1115 1925 (All directory operations take place in a reserved area that does not affect write buffers as was the) 1925 SB
225 1174 1821 (case in release 1, with the exception of Search First and Search Next, where compatibility is) 1821 SB
225 1233 178 (required.) 178 SB
225 1351 1842 (The FCB data area consists of a sequence of 33 bytes for sequential access and a series of 36) 1842 SB
225 1410 1784 (bytes in the case when the file is accessed randomly. The default FCB, normally located at) 1784 SB
225 1470 1889 (005CH, can be used for random access files, because the three bytes starting at BOOT+007DH) 1889 SB
225 1529 1787 (are available for this purpose.  Figure 5-2 shows the FCB format with the following fields.) 1787 SB
