225 227 1833 (Each file being accessed through CP/M must have a corresponding FCB, which provides the) 1833 SB
225 286 1915 (name and allocation information for all subsequent file operations. When  accessing files, it is the) 1915 SB
225 345 1778 (programmer's responsibility to fill the lower 16 bytes of the FCB and initialize the cr field.) 1778 SB
225 405 1911 (Normally, bytes 1 through 11 are set to the ASCII character values for the filename and filetype,) 1911 SB
225 464 587 (while all other fields are zero.) 587 SB
225 582 1882 (FCBs are stored in a directory area of the disk, and are brought into central memory before the) 1882 SB
225 641 1874 (programmer proceeds with file operations \(see the OPEN and MAKE functions\). The memory) 1874 SB
225 700 1945 (copy of the FCB is updated as file operations take place and later recorded permanently on disk at) 1945 SB
225 760 1291 (the termination of the file operation, \(see the CLOSE command\).) 1291 SB
225 878 1797 (The CCP constructs the first 16 bytes of two optional FCBs for a transient by scanning the) 1797 SB
225 937 1842 (remainder of the line following the transient name, denoted by file1 and file2 in the prototype) 1842 SB
225 996 1824 (command line described above, with unspecified fields set to ASCII blanks. The first FCB is) 1824 SB
225 1055 1903 (constructed at location BOOT+005CH and can be used as is for subsequent file operations. The) 1903 SB
225 1115 1930 (second FCB occupies the d0 ... dn portion of the first FCB and must be moved to another area of) 1930 SB
225 1174 1470 (memory before use. If, for example, the following command line is typed: ) 1470 SB
225 1292 640 (PROGNAME B:X.ZOT Y.ZAP) 640 SB
225 1410 1854 (the file PROGNAME.COM is loaded into the TPA, and the default FCB at BOOT+005CH is) 1854 SB
225 1470 1924 (initialized to drive code 2, filename X, and filetype ZOT. The second drive code takes the default) 1924 SB
225 1529 1661 (value 0, which is placed at BOOT+006CH, with the filename Y placed into location) 1661 SB
225 1588 1846 (BOOT+006DH and filetype ZAP located 8 bytes later at BOOT+0075H. All remaining fields) 1846 SB
225 1647 1814 (through cr are set to zero. Note again that it is the programmer's responsibility to move this) 1814 SB
225 1706 1923 (second filename and filetype to another area, usually a separate file control block, before opening) 1923 SB
225 1765 1868 (the file that begins at BOOT+005CH, because the open operation overwrites the second name) 1868 SB
225 1825 182 (and type.) 182 SB
225 1943 1914 (If no filenames are specified in the original command, the fields beginning at BOOT+005DH and) 1914 SB
225 2002 1759 (BOOT+006DH contain blanks. In all cases, the CCP translates lower-case alphabetics to) 1759 SB
225 2061 1341 (upper-case to be consistent with the CP/M file naming conventions.) 1341 SB
225 92 417 (5.2 Call Conventions) 417 SB
