225 1825 1806 (Direct Console I/O is supported under CP/M for those specialized applications where basic) 1806 SB
225 1884 1917 (console input and output are required. Use of this function should, in general, be avoided since it) 1917 SB
225 1943 1736 (bypasses all of the CP/M normal control character functions \(for example, CTRL-S and) 1736 SB
225 2002 1919 (CTRL-P\). Programs that perform direct I/O through the BIOS under previous releases of CP/M,) 1919 SB
225 2061 1871 (however, should be changed to use direct I/O under BDOS so that they can be fully supported) 1871 SB
225 2120 839 (under future releases of MP/M and CP/M.) 839 SB
225 2239 1862 (Upon entry to Function 6, register E either contains hexadecimal FF, denoting a console input) 1862 SB
225 2298 1929 (request, or an ASCII character. If the input value is FF, Function 6 returns A = 00 if no character) 1929 SB
225 2357 1256 (is ready, otherwise A contains the next console input character.) 1256 SB
225 2475 1916 (If the input value in E is not FF, Function 6 assumes that E contains a valid ASCII character that) 1916 SB
225 2535 429 (is sent to the console.) 429 SB
225 2653 1526 (Function 6 must not be used in conjunction with other console I/O functions.) 1526 SB
