225 227 1880 (Thus, the r0, r1 byte pair is treated as a double-byte, or word value, that contains the record to) 1880 SB
225 286 1759 (read. This value ranges from 0 to 65535, providing access to any particular record of the) 1759 SB
225 345 1870 (8-megabyte file. To process a file using random access, the base extent \(extent 0\) must first be) 1870 SB
225 405 1925 (opened. Although the base extent might or might not contain any allocated data, this ensures that) 1925 SB
225 464 1871 (the file is properly recorded in the directory and is visible in DIR requests. The selected record) 1871 SB
225 523 1829 (number is then stored in the random record field \(r0, r1\), and the BDOS is called to read the) 1829 SB
225 582 142 (record.) 142 SB
225 700 1890 (Upon return from the call, register A either contains an error code, as listed below, or the value) 1890 SB
225 760 1912 (00, indicating the operation was successful. In the latter case, the current DMA address contains) 1912 SB
225 819 1850 (the randomly accessed record. Note that contrary to the sequential read operation, the record) 1850 SB
225 878 1848 (number is not advanced. Thus, subsequent random read operations continue to read the same) 1848 SB
225 937 155 (record. ) 155 SB
225 1055 1915 (Upon each random read operation, the logical extent and current record values are automatically) 1915 SB
225 1115 1934 (set. Thus, the file can be sequentially read or written, starting from the current randomly accessed) 1934 SB
225 1174 1842 (position. However, note that, in this case, the last randomly read record will be reread as one) 1842 SB
225 1233 1785 (switches from random mode to sequential read and the last record will be rewritten as one) 1785 SB
225 1292 1933 (switches to a sequential write operation. The user can simply advance the random record position) 1933 SB
225 1351 1686 (following each random read or write to obtain the effect of sequential I/O operation. ) 1686 SB
225 1470 1511 (Error codes returned in register A following a random read are listed below.) 1511 SB
225 1588 510 (01 reading unwritten data) 510 SB
225 1647 678 (02 \(not returned in random mode\)) 678 SB
225 1706 600 (03 cannot close current extent) 600 SB
225 1765 543 (04 seek to unwritten extent) 543 SB
225 1825 613 (05 \(not returned in read mode\)) 613 SB
225 1884 655 (06 seek Past Physical end of disk) 655 SB
225 2002 1868 (Error codes 01 and 04 occur when a random read operation accesses a data block that has not) 1868 SB
225 2061 1887 (been previously written or an extent that has not been created, which are equivalent conditions.) 1887 SB
225 2120 1945 (Error code 03 does not normally occur under proper system operation. If it does, it can be cleared) 1945 SB
225 2180 1923 (by simply rereading or reopening extent zero as long as the disk is not physically write protected.) 1923 SB
225 2239 1801 (Error code 06 occurs whenever byte r2 is nonzero under the current 2.0 release. Normally,) 1801 SB
225 2298 1905 (nonzero return codes can be treated as missing data, with zero return codes indicating operation) 1905 SB
225 2357 194 (complete.) 194 SB
