225 1055 1932 (When computing the size of a file, the DE register pair addresses an FCB in random mode format) 1932 SB
225 1115 1904 (\(bytes r0, r1, and r2 are present\). The FCB contains an unambiguous filename that is used in the) 1904 SB
225 1174 1842 (directory scan. Upon return, the random record bytes contain the virtual file size, which is, in) 1842 SB
225 1233 1917 (effect, the record address of the record following the end of the file. Following a call to Function) 1917 SB
225 1292 1942 (35, if the high record byte r2 is 01, the file contains the maximum record count 65536. Otherwise,) 1942 SB
225 1351 1940 (bytes r0 and r1 constitute a 16-bit value as before \(r0 is the least significant byte\), which is the file) 1940 SB
225 1410 88 (size.) 88 SB
225 1529 1794 (Data can be appended to the end of an existing file by simply calling Function 35 to set the) 1794 SB
225 1588 1809 (random record position to the end-of-file and then performing a sequence of random writes) 1809 SB
225 1647 723 (starting at the preset record address.) 723 SB
225 1765 1880 (The virtual size of a file corresponds to the physical size when the file is written sequentially. If) 1880 SB
225 1825 1923 (the file was created in random mode and holes exist in the allocation, the file might contain fewer) 1923 SB
225 1884 1945 (records than the size indicates. For example, if only the last record of an 8-megabyte file is written) 1945 SB
225 1943 1900 (in random mode \(that is, record number 65535\), the virtual size is 65536 records, although only) 1900 SB
225 2002 759 (one block of data is actually allocated.) 759 SB
