225 937 1918 (The Set Random Record function causes the BDOS automatically to produce the random record) 1918 SB
225 996 1888 (position from a file that has been read or written sequentially to a particular point. The function) 1888 SB
225 1055 523 (can be useful in two ways.) 523 SB
225 1174 1842 (First, it is often necessary initially to read and scan a sequential file to extract the positions of) 1842 SB
225 1233 1839 (various key fields.  As each key is encountered, Function 36 is called to compute the random) 1839 SB
225 1292 1814 (record position for the data corresponding to this key.  If the data unit size is 128 bytes, the) 1814 SB
225 1351 1897 (resulting record position is placed into a table with the key for later retrieval. After scanning the) 1897 SB
225 1410 1819 (entire file and tabulating the keys and their record numbers, the user can move instantly to a) 1819 SB
225 1470 1860 (particular keyed record by performing a random read, using the corresponding random record) 1860 SB
225 1529 1805 (number that was saved earlier. The scheme is easily generalized for variable record lengths,) 1805 SB
225 1588 1828 (because the program need only store the buffer-relative byte position along with the key and) 1828 SB
225 1647 1626 (record number to find the exact starting position of the keyed data at a later time. ) 1626 SB
225 1765 1815 (A second use of Function 36 occurs when switching from a sequential read or write over to) 1815 SB
225 1825 1943 (random read or write. A file is sequentially accessed to a particular point in the file, Function 36 is) 1943 SB
225 1884 1921 (called, which sets the record number, and subsequent random read and write operations continue) 1921 SB
225 1943 666 (from the selected point in the file.) 666 SB
