84 13 SJ
255 345 1836 (The following program provides a relatively simple example of file operations.  The program) 1836 SB
2 15 SJ
225 405 1948 (source file is created as COPY.ASM using the CP/M ED program and then assembled using ASM) 1948 SB
16 18 SJ
225 464 1934 (or MAC, resulting in a HEX file.  The LOAD program is used to produce a COPY.COM file that) 1934 SB
32 17 SJ
225 523 1918 (executes directly under the CCP.  The program begins bv setting the stack pointer to a local area) 1918 SB
30 17 SJ
225 582 1920 (and proceeds to move the second name from the default area at 006CH to a 33-byte File Control) 1920 SB
8 16 SJ
225 641 1942 (Block called DFCB.  The DFCB is then prepared for file operations by clearing the current record) 1942 SB
27 16 SJ
225 700 1923 (field.  At this point, the source and destination FCBs are ready for processing, because the SFCB) 1923 SB
5 20 SJ
225 760 1945 (at 005CH is properly set up by the CCP upon entry to the COPY program.  That is, the first name) 1945 SB
35 16 SJ
225 819 1915 (is placed into the default FCB, with the proper fields zeroed, including the current record field at) 1915 SB
36 14 SJ
225 878 1914 (007CH.  The program continues by opening the source file, deleting any existing destination file,) 1914 SB
95 17 SJ
225 937 1855 (and creating the destination file.  If all this is successful, the program loops at the label COPY) 1855 SB
232 16 SJ
225 996 1718 (until each record is read from the source file and placed into the destination file.  Upon) 1718 SB
40 16 SJ
225 1055 1910 (completion of the data transfer, the destination file is closed and the program returns to the CCP) 1910 SB
225 1115 747 (command level by jumping to BOOT.) 747 SB
