225 1647 1949 (Note that there are several simplifications in this particular program.  First, there are no checks for) 1949 SB
225 1706 1873 (invalid filenames that could contain ambiguous references.  This situation could be detected by) 1873 SB
225 1765 1926 (scanning the 32-byte default area starting at location 005CH for ASCII question marks.  A check) 1926 SB
225 1825 1928 (should also be made to ensure that the filenames have been included \(check locations 005DH and) 1928 SB
225 1884 1815 (006DH for nonblank ASCII characters\).  Finally, a check should be made to ensure that the) 1815 SB
225 1943 1868 (source and destination filenames are different.  An improvement in speed could be obtained by) 1868 SB
225 2002 1801 (buffering more data on each read operation.  One could, for example, determine the size of) 1801 SB
225 2061 1811 (memory by fetching FBASE from location 0006H and using the entire remaining portion of) 1811 SB
225 2120 1862 (memory for a data buffer.  In this case, the programmer simply resets the DMA address to the) 1862 SB
225 2180 1880 (next successive 128-byte area before each read.  Upon writing to the destination file, the DMA) 1880 SB
225 2239 1563 (address is reset to the beginning of the buffer and incremented by 128 bytes to ) 1563 SB
1788 2239 302 (the end as each) 302 SB
225 2298 845 (record is transferred to the destination file.) 845 SB
