225 227 787 (5.5 A Sample Random Access Program) 787 SB
225 345 1839 (This section concludes with an extensive example of random access operation.  The program) 1839 SB
225 405 1887 (listed below performs the simple function of reading or writing random records upon command) 1887 SB
225 464 1919 (from the terminal.  When a program has been created, assembled, and placed into a file labeled R) 1919 SB
225 523 913 (A N D 0 M . C 0 M , the CCP level command) 913 SB
225 641 397 (RANDOM X. DAT) 397 SB
225 760 1779 (starts the test program.  The program looks for a file by the name X . DA T and, if found,) 1779 SB
225 819 1868 (proceeds to prompt the console for input.  If not found, the file is created before the prompt is) 1868 SB
225 878 701 (given.  Each prompt takes the form) 701 SB
315 996 312 (next command?) 312 SB
225 1115 1885 (and is followed by operator input, followed by a carriage return.  The input commands take the) 1885 SB
225 1174 95 (form) 95 SB
315 1292 163 (nWnRQ) 163 SB
225 1410 1786 (where n is an integer value in the range 0 to 65535, and W, R, and Q are simple command) 1786 SB
225 1470 1925 (characters corresponding to random write, random read, and quit processing, respectively.  If the) 1925 SB
225 1529 1294 (W command is issued, the RANDOM program issues the prompt) 1294 SB
225 1647 196 (type data:) 196 SB
225 1765 1778 (The operator then responds by typing up to 127 characters, followed by a carriage return.) 1778 SB
225 1825 1950 (RANDOM then writes the character string into the "', . DA T file at record n. If the R command is) 1950 SB
225 1884 1867 (issued, RANDOM reads record number n and displays the string value at the console, If the Q) 1867 SB
225 1943 1800 (command is issued, the X . DAT file is closed, and the program returns to the CCP.  In the) 1800 SB
225 2002 870 (interest of brevity, the only error message is) 870 SB
225 2120 324 (error, try again .) 324 SB
161 14 SJ
225 2239 1789 (The program begins with an initialization section where the input file is opened or created,) 1789 SB
42 14 SJ
225 2298 1908 (followed by a continuous loop at the label ready where the individual commands are interpreted.) 1908 SB
43 18 SJ
225 2357 1907 (The DFBC at 005CH and the default buffer at 0080H are used in all disk operations.  The utility) 1907 SB
134 13 SJ
225 2416 1816 (subroutines then follow, which contain the principal input line processor, called readc.  This) 1816 SB
23 15 SJ
225 2475 1927 (particular program shows the elements of random access processing, and can be used as the basis) 1927 SB
225 2535 668 (for further program development.) 668 SB
225 92 787 (5.5 A Sample Random Access Program) 787 SB
1425 92 649 (CP/M Operating System Manual) 649 SB
