225 1115 1904 (Major improvements could be made to this particular program to enhance its operation.  In fact,) 1904 SB
225 1174 1859 (with some work, this program could evolve into a simple data base management system.  One) 1859 SB
225 1233 1824 (could, for example, assume a standard record size of 128 bytes, consisting to arbitrary fields) 1824 SB
225 1292 1904 (within the record.  A program, called GETKEY, could be developed that first reads a sequential) 1904 SB
225 1351 1664 (file and extracts a specific field defined by the operator.  For example, the command) 1664 SB
225 1470 911 (GETKEY NAMES.DAT LASTNAME 10 20) 911 SB
225 1588 1950 (would cause GETKEY to read the data base file NAMES.DAT and extract the LASTNAME field) 1950 SB
225 1647 1885 (from each record, starting in position 10 and ending at character 20. GETKEY builds a table in) 1885 SB
225 1706 1843 (memory consisting of each particular LASTNAME field, along with its 16-bit record number) 1843 SB
225 1765 1878 (location within the file.  The GETKEY program then sorts this list and writes a new file, called) 1878 SB
225 1825 1905 (LASTNAME.KEY, which is an alphabetical list of LASTNAME fields with their corresponding) 1905 SB
225 1884 1691 (record numbers.  This list is called an inverted index in information retrieval parlance.) 1691 SB
225 2002 1701 (If the programmer were to rename the program shown above as QUERY and modify ) 1701 SB
1926 2002 214 (it so that it) 214 SB
225 2061 1414 (reads a sorted key file into memory, the command line might appear as ) 1414 SB
225 2180 872 (QUERY NAMES.DAT LASTNAME.KEY) 872 SB
