225 286 1756 (Instead of reading a number, the QUERY program reads an alphanumeric string that is a) 1756 SB
39 30 SJ
225 345 1911 (particular key to find in the N A M E S . D A T data base.  Because the L A S T N A M E . K E) 1911 SB
81 17 SJ
225 405 1869 (Y list is sorted, one can find a particular entry rapidly by performing a binary search, similar to) 1869 SB
10 19 SJ
225 464 1940 (looking up a name in the telephone book.  Starting at both ends of the list, one examines the entry) 1940 SB
49 18 SJ
225 523 1901 (halfway in between and, if not matched, splits either the upper half or the lower half for the next) 1901 SB
82 16 SJ
225 582 1868 (search.  You will quickly reach the item you are looking for and find the corresponding record) 1868 SB
