225 405 348 (6.1 Introduction) 348 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c322 font
225 523 1938 (The standard CP/M system assumes operation on an Intel MDS-800 microcomputer development) 1938 SB
225 582 1838 (system, but is designed so you can alter a specific set of subroutines that define the hardware) 1838 SB
225 641 461 (operating environment.) 461 SB
225 760 1910 ( Although standard CP/M 2 is configured for single-density floppy disks, field alteration features) 1910 SB
225 819 1948 (allow adaptation to a wide variety of disk subsystems from single drive minidisks to high-capacity,) 1948 SB
225 878 1932 (hard disk systems. To simplify the following adaptation process, it is assumed that CP/M 2 is first) 1932 SB
225 937 1760 (configured for single-density floppy disks where minimal editing and debugging tools are) 1760 SB
225 996 1941 (available. If an earlier version of CP/M is available, the customizing process is eased considerably.) 1941 SB
225 1055 1814 (In this latter case, you might want to review the system generation process and skip to later) 1814 SB
225 1115 1351 (sections that discuss system alteration for nonstandard disk systems.) 1351 SB
225 1233 1584 (To achieve device independence, CP/M is separated into three distinct modules:) 1584 SB
225 1351 1324 (   -BIOS is the Basic I/O System, which is environment dependent.) 1324 SB
225 1470 1782 (   -BDOS is the Basic Disk Operating System, which is not dependent upon the hardware ) 1782 SB
225 1529 343 (     configuration.) 343 SB
225 1647 1344 (   -CCP is the Console Command Processor, which uses the BDOS.) 1344 SB
225 1765 1894 (Of these modules, only the BIOS is dependent upon the particular hardware. You can patch the) 1894 SB
225 1825 1773 (distribution version of CP/M to provide a new BIOS that provides a customized interface) 1773 SB
225 1884 1801 (between the remaining CP/M modules and the hardware system. This document provides a) 1801 SB
225 1943 1202 (step-by-step procedure for patching a new BIOS into CP/M.) 1202 SB
225 2061 1900 (All disk-dependent portions of CP/M 2 are placed into a BIOS, a resident disk parameter block,) 1900 SB
225 2120 1825 (which is either hand coded or produced automatically using the disk definition macro library) 1825 SB
225 2180 1911 (provided with CP/M 2. The end user need only specify the maximum number of active disks, the) 1911 SB
225 2239 1872 (starting and ending sector numbers, the data allocation size, the maximum extent of the logical) 1872 SB
225 2298 1864 (disk, directory size information, and reserved track values. The macros use this information to) 1864 SB
225 2357 1935 (generate the appropriate tables and table references for use during CP/M 2 operation. Deblocking) 1935 SB
225 2416 1945 (information is provided, which aids in assembly or disassembly of sector sizes that are multiples of) 1945 SB
225 2475 1869 (the fundamental 128-byte data unit, and the system alteration manual includes general purpose) 1869 SB
225 2535 1888 (subroutines that use the deblocking information to take advantage of larger sector sizes. Use of) 1888 SB
225 2594 1938 (these subroutines, together with the table-drive data access algorithms, makes CP/M 2 a universal) 1938 SB
225 2653 508 (data management system.) 508 SB
1166 2942 67 (6-1) 67 SB
