SS
0 0 25 25 798 1100 300 SM
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c322 font
0 0 0 fC
225 227 1883 (File expansion is achieved by providing up to 512 logical file extents, where each logical extent) 1883 SB
225 286 1863 (contains 16K bytes of data.  CP/M 2 is structured, however, so that as much as 128K bytes of) 1863 SB
225 345 1741 (data are addressed by a single physical extent, corresponding to a single directory entry,) 1741 SB
225 405 1809 (iuaintaining compatibility with previous versions while taking advantage of directory space.) 1809 SB
225 523 1864 (If CP/M is being tailored to a computer system for the first time, the new BIOS requires some) 1864 SB
225 582 1908 (simple software development and testing. The standard BIOS is listed in Appendix A and can be) 1908 SB
225 641 1929 (used as a model for the customized package. A skeletal version of the BIOS given in Appendix B) 1929 SB
225 700 855 (can serve as the basis for a modified BIOS.) 855 SB
225 819 1903 (In addition to the BIOS, you must write a simple memory loader, called GETSYS, which brings) 1903 SB
225 878 1808 (the operating system into memory.  To patch the new BIOS into CP/M, you must write the) 1808 SB
225 937 1854 (reverse of GETSYS, called PUTSYS, which places an altered version of CP/M back onto the) 1854 SB
225 996 1947 (disk. PUTSYS can be derived from GETSYS by changing the disk read commands into disk write) 1947 SB
225 1055 1893 (commands.  Sample skeletal GETSYS and PUTSYS programs are described in Section 6.4 and) 1893 SB
225 1115 418 (listed in Appendix C.) 418 SB
225 1233 1931 (To make the CP/M system load automatically, you must also supply a cold start loader, similar to) 1931 SB
225 1292 1930 (the one provided with CP/M, listed in Appendixes A and D. A skeletal form of a cold start loader) 1930 SB
225 1351 1248 (is given in Appendix E, which serves as a model for the loader.) 1248 SB
