225 227 64 (  3.) 64 SB
375 227 1782 (Run the GETSYS program using an initialized CP/M disk to see if GETSYS loads CP/M ) 1782 SB
375 286 1608 (starting at 3380H \(the operating system actually starts 128 bytes later at 3400H\).) 1608 SB
225 345 13 ( ) 13 SB
225 405 64 (  4.) 64 SB
375 405 1790 (Read Section 6.4 and write the PUTSYS program. This writes memory starting at 3380H ) 1790 SB
375 464 1715 (back onto the first two tracks of the disk. The PUTSYS program should be located at ) 1715 SB
375 523 633 (200H, as shown in Appendix C.) 633 SB
225 582 13 ( ) 13 SB
225 641 64 (  5.) 64 SB
375 641 1649 (Test the PUTSYS program using a blank, uninitialized disk by writing a portion of ) 1649 SB
375 700 1676 (memory to the first two tracks; clear memory and read it back using GETSYS. Test ) 1676 SB
375 760 1568 (PUTSYS completely, because this program will be used to alter CP/M on disk.) 1568 SB
225 878 64 (  6.) 64 SB
375 878 1764 (Study Sections 6.5, 6.6, and 6.7 along with the distribution version of the BIOS given in ) 1764 SB
375 937 1789 (Appendix A and write a simple version that performs a similar function for the customized) 1789 SB
375 996 1746 (environment. Use the program given in Appendix B as a model.  Call this new BIOS by ) 1746 SB
375 1055 1688 (name CBIOS \(customized BIOS\). Implement only the primitive disk operations on a ) 1688 SB
375 1115 1339 (single drive and simple console input/output functions in this phase.) 1339 SB
225 1233 64 (  7.) 64 SB
375 1233 1790 (Test CBIOS completely to ensure that it properly performs console character I/O and disk) 1790 SB
375 1292 1708 (reads and writes. Be careful to ensure that no disk write operations occur during read ) 1708 SB
