225 1055 1855 (The sector allocation for the standard distribution version of CP/M is given here for reference) 1855 SB
225 1115 1771 (purposes. The first sector contains an optional software boot section \(see the table on the) 1771 SB
225 1174 1845 (following page\). Disk controllers are often set up to bring track 0, sector 1, into memory at a) 1845 SB
225 1233 1776 (specific location, often location 0000H. The program in this sector, called BOOT, has the) 1776 SB
225 1292 1935 (responsibility of bringing the remaining sectors into memory starting at location 3400H + b. If the) 1935 SB
225 1351 1928 (controller does not have a built-in sector load, the program in track 0, sector 1 can be ignored. In) 1928 SB
225 1410 1472 (this case, load the program from track 0, sector 2, to location 3400H + b. ) 1472 SB
225 1529 1801 (As an example, the Intel MDS-800 hardware cold start loader brings track 0, sector 1, into) 1801 SB
225 1588 1936 (absolute address 3000H. Upon loading this sector, control transfers to location 3000H, where the) 1936 SB
225 1647 1784 (bootstrap operation commences by loading the remainder of track 0 and all of track 1 into) 1784 SB
225 1706 1824 (memory, starting at 3400H + b. Note that this bootstrap loader is of little use in a non-MDS) 1824 SB
225 1765 1905 (environment, although it is useful to examine it because some of the boot actions will have to be) 1905 SB
225 1825 800 (duplicated in the user's cold start loader.) 800 SB
