225 227 1717 (All simple character I/O operations are assumed to be performed in ASCII, upper- and) 1717 SB
225 286 1930 (lower-case, with high-order \(parity bit\) set to zero. An end-of-file condition for an input device is) 1930 SB
225 345 1892 (given by an ASCII CTRL-Z \(1AH\). Peripheral devices are seen by CP/M as logical devices and) 1892 SB
225 405 990 (are assigned to physical devices within the BIOS. ) 990 SB
225 523 1814 (To operate, the BDOS needs only the CONST, CONIN, and CONOUT subroutines. LIST,) 1814 SB
225 582 1852 (PUNCH, and READER can be used by PIP, but not the BDOS. Further, the LISTST entry is) 1852 SB
225 641 1884 (currently used only by DESPOOL, the print spooling utility. Thus, the initial version of CBIOS) 1884 SB
225 700 1217 (can have empty subroutines for the remaining ASCII devices.) 1217 SB
225 819 1070 (The following list describes the characteristics of each) 1070 SB
225 878 140 (device.) 140 SB
225 996 39 (   ) 39 SB
375 996 1799 (-CONSOLE is the principal interactive console that communicates with the operator and it) 1799 SB
375 1055 1721 (  is accessed through CONST, CONIN, and CONOUT. Typically, the CONSOLE is a ) 1721 SB
375 1115 693 (  device such as a CRT or teletype.) 693 SB
225 1233 39 (   ) 39 SB
375 1233 1623 (-LIST is the principal listing device. If it exists on the user's system, it is usually a ) 1623 SB
375 1292 955 (  hard-copy device, such as a printer or teletype.) 955 SB
225 1410 39 (   ) 39 SB
375 1410 1700 (-PUNCH is the principal tape punching device. If it exists, it is normally a high-speed ) 1700 SB
375 1470 605 (  paper tape punch or teletype.) 605 SB
225 1588 13 ( ) 13 SB
375 1588 1783 (-READER is the principal tape reading device, such as a simple optical reader or teletype.) 1783 SB
225 1706 1949 (A single peripheral can be assigned as the LIST, PUNCH, and READER device simultaneously. If) 1949 SB
225 1765 1910 (no peripheral device is assigned as the LIST, PUNCH, or READER device, the CBIOS gives an) 1910 SB
225 1825 1865 (appropriate error message so that the system does not hang if the device is accessed by PIP or) 1865 SB
225 1884 1867 (some other user program.  Alternately, the PUNCH and LIST routines can \)'ust simply return,) 1867 SB
225 1943 1900 (and the READER routine can return with a 1 AH \(CTRL-Z\) in register A to indicate immediate) 1900 SB
225 2002 220 (end-of-file.) 220 SB
225 2120 1739 (For added flexibility, you can optionally implement the IOBYTE function, which allows) 1739 SB
225 2180 1921 (reassignment of physical devices. The IOBYTE function creates a mapping of logical-to-physical) 1921 SB
225 2239 1865 (devices that can be altered during CP/M processing, see the STAT command in Section 1.6.1.) 1865 SB
225 92 450 (6.6 BIOS Entry Points) 450 SB
1526 92 649 (CP/M Operating System Manual) 649 SB
