225 1292 1899 (The implementation of the IOBYTE is optional and effects only the organization of the CBIOS.) 1899 SB
225 1351 1819 (No CP/M systems use the IOBYTE \(although they tolerate the existence of the IOBYTE at) 1819 SB
225 1410 1886 (location 0003H\) except for PIP, which allows access to the physical devices, and STAT, which) 1886 SB
225 1470 1927 (allows logical-physical assignments to be made or displayed. For more information see Section 1.) 1927 SB
225 1529 1769 (In any case the IOBYTE implementation should be omitted until the basic CBIOS is fully) 1769 SB
225 1588 1656 (implemented and tested; then you should add the IOBYTE to increase the facilities.) 1656 SB
225 1706 1903 (Disk I/O is always performed through a sequence of calls on the various disk access subroutines) 1903 SB
225 1765 1852 (that set up the disk number to access, the track and sector on a particular disk, and the Direct) 1852 SB
225 1825 1872 (Memory Access \(DMA\) address involved in the I/O operation. After all these parameters have) 1872 SB
225 1884 1921 (been set up, a call is made to the READ or WRITE function to perform the actual I/O operation.) 1921 SB
225 2002 1860 (There is often a single call to SELDSK to select a disk drive, followed by a number of read or) 1860 SB
225 2061 1856 (write operations to the selected disk before selecting another drive for subsequent operations.) 1856 SB
225 2120 1915 (Similarly, there might be a single call to set the DMA address, followed by several calls that read) 1915 SB
225 2180 1827 (or write from the selected DMA address before the DMA address is changed. The track and) 1827 SB
225 2239 1826 (sector subroutines are always called before the READ or WRITE operations are performed.) 1826 SB
225 92 450 (6.6 BIOS Entry Points) 450 SB
1526 92 649 (CP/M Operating System Manual) 649 SB
1154 2942 92 (6-19) 92 SB
