225 345 1767 (The program shown in Appendix B can serve as a basis for your first BIOS. The simplest) 1767 SB
225 405 1876 (functions are assumed in this BIOS, so that you can enter it through a front panel, if absolutely) 1876 SB
225 464 1911 (necessary. You must alter and insert code into the subroutines for CONST, CONIN, CONOUT,) 1911 SB
225 523 1892 (READ, WRITE, and WAITIO subroutines.  Storage is reserved for user-supplied code in these) 1892 SB
225 582 1835 (regions. The scratch area reserved in page zero \(see Section 6.9\) for the BIOS is used in this) 1835 SB
225 641 1213 (program, so that it could be implemented in ROM, if desired.) 1213 SB
225 760 1872 (Once operational, this skeletal version can be enhanced to print the initial sign-on message and) 1872 SB
225 819 1887 (perform better error recovery.  The subroutines for LIST, PUNCH, and READER can be filled) 1887 SB
225 878 1016 (out and the IOBYTE function can be implemented.) 1016 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 46 /MSTT31c356 font
225 996 685 (6.8 A Sample Cold Start Loader) 685 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c322 font
225 1115 1833 (The program shown in Appendix E can serve as a basis for a cold start loader. The disk read) 1833 SB
225 1174 1820 (function must be supplied by the user, and the program must be loaded somehow starting at) 1820 SB
225 1233 1893 (location 0000. Space is reserved for the patch code so that the total amount of storage required) 1893 SB
225 1292 729 (for the cold start loader is 128 bytes.) 729 SB
225 1410 1847 (Eventually, you might want to get this loader onto the first disk sector \(track 0, sector 1\) and) 1847 SB
225 1470 1918 (cause the controller to load it into memory automatically upon system start up. Alternatively, the) 1918 SB
225 1529 1950 (cold start loader can be placed into ROM, and above the CP/M system. In this case, it is necessary) 1950 SB
225 1588 1907 (to originate the program at a higher address and key in a jump instruction at system start up that) 1907 SB
225 1647 1911 (branches to the loader. Subsequent warm starts do not require this key-in operation, because the) 1911 SB
225 1706 1812 (entry point WBOOT gets control, thus bringing the system in from disk automatically.  The) 1812 SB
225 1765 1896 (skeletal cold start loader has minimal error recovery, which might be enhanced in later versions.) 1896 SB
225 92 283 (6.7 A Sample ) 283 SB
