975 937 314 (128 times # bits) 314 SB
525 996 386 (                8,192     ) 386 SB
975 996 314 (256 times # bits) 314 SB
525 1055 398 (                16,384    ) 398 SB
975 1055 314 (512 times # bits) 314 SB
225 1174 1883 (Thus, if DRM = 127 \(128 directory entries\) and BLS = 1024, there are 32 directory entries per) 1883 SB
225 1233 1898 (block, requiring 4 reserved blocks. In this case, the 4 high-order bits of AL0 are set, resulting in) 1898 SB
225 1292 808 (the values AL0 = 0F0H and AL1 = 00H.) 808 SB
225 1410 1928 (The CKS value is determined as follows: if the disk drive media is removable, then CKS = \(DRM) 1928 SB
225 1470 1878 (+ 1\)/4, where DRM is the last directory entry number. If the media are fixed, then set CKS = 0) 1878 SB
225 1529 929 (\(no directory records are checked in this case\).) 929 SB
225 1647 1858 (Finally, the OFF field determines the number of tracks that are skipped at the beginning of the) 1858 SB
225 1706 1932 (physical disk. This value is automatically added whenever SETTRK is called and can be used as a) 1932 SB
225 1765 1832 (mechanism for skipping reserved operating system tracks or for partitioning a large disk into) 1832 SB
225 1825 550 (smaller segmented sections.) 550 SB
225 1943 1871 (To complete the discussion of the DPB, several DPHs can address the same DPB if their drive) 1871 SB
225 2002 1888 (characteristics are identical.  Further, the DPB can be dynamically changed when a new drive is) 1888 SB
225 2061 1914 (addressed by simply changing the pointer in the DPH; because the BDOS copies the DPB values) 1914 SB
225 2120 1162 (to a local area whenever the SELDSK function is invoked.) 1162 SB
225 2239 1946 (Returning back to DPH for a particular drive, the two address values CSV and ALV remain. Both) 1946 SB
225 2298 1810 (addresses reference an area of uninitialized memory following the BIOS. The areas must be) 1810 SB
225 2357 1727 (unique for each drive, and the size of each area is determined by the values in the DPB.) 1727 SB
