225 878 753 (6.11 The DISKDEF Macro Library) 753 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c322 font
225 996 1935 (A macro library called DISKDEF \(shown in Appendix F\), greatly simplifies the table construction) 1935 SB
225 1055 1867 (process. You must have access to the MAC macro assembler, of course, to use the DISKDEF) 1867 SB
225 1115 1534 (facility, while the macro library is included with all CP/M 2 distribution disks.) 1534 SB
225 1233 1576 (A BIOS disk definition consists of the following sequence of macro statements:) 1576 SB
225 1351 545 (     MACLIB      DISKDEF) 545 SB
225 1470 324 (     DISKS       n) 324 SB
225 1529 443 (     DISKDEF     0,. . .) 443 SB
225 1588 443 (     DISKDEF     1,. . .) 443 SB
225 1647 195 (          .....) 195 SB
225 1706 432 (     DISKDEF     n - 1) 432 SB
225 1825 226 (     ENDEF) 226 SB
225 1943 1900 (where the MACLIB statement loads the DISKDEF.LIB file, on the same disk as the BIOS, into) 1900 SB
225 2002 1924 (MAC's internal tables. The DISKS macro call follows, which specifies the number of drives to be) 1924 SB
225 2061 1735 (configured with the user's system, where n is an integer in the range 1 to 16. A series of) 1735 SB
225 2120 1928 (DISKDEF macro calls then follow that define the characteristics of each logical disk, 0 through n) 1928 SB
225 2180 1932 (- 1, corresponding to logical drives A through P. The DISKS and DISKDEF macros generate the) 1932 SB
225 2239 1691 (in-line fixed data tables described in the previous section and thus must be placed in a) 1691 SB
225 2298 1716 (nonexecutable portion of the BIOS, typically directly following the BIOS jump vector.) 1716 SB
225 92 551 (6.10 Disk Parameter Tables) 551 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c322 font
1526 92 649 (CP/M Operating System Manual) 649 SB
1154 2942 92 (6-34) 92 SB
