0 0 25 25 798 1100 300 SM
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c322 font
0 0 0 fC
225 227 1915 (The remaining portion of the BIOS is defined following the DISKDEF macros, with the ENDEF) 1915 SB
225 286 1820 (macro call immediately preceding the END statement. The ENDEF \(End of Diskdef\) macro) 1820 SB
225 345 1846 (generates the necessary uninitialized RAM areas that are located in memory above the BIOS.) 1846 SB
225 464 830 (The DISKDEF macro call takes the form:) 830 SB
225 582 1019 (     DISKDEF dn,fsc,lsc,[skf],bls dks,dir,cks,ofs,[0]) 1019 SB
225 700 121 (where) 121 SB
225 760 846 (   -dn is the logical disk number, 0 to n - 1.) 846 SB
225 819 968 (   -fsc is the first physical sector number \(0 or 1\).) 968 SB
225 878 617 (   -lsc is the last sector number.) 617 SB
225 937 800 (   -skf is the optional sector skew factor.) 800 SB
225 996 755 (   -bls is the data allocation block size.) 755 SB
225 1055 844 (   -dks is the number of blocks on the disk.) 844 SB
225 1115 793 (   -dir is the number of directory entries.) 793 SB
225 1174 993 (   -cks is the number of checked directory entries. ) 993 SB
225 1233 861 (   -ofs is the track offset to logical track 00.) 861 SB
225 1292 839 (   -[0] is an optional 1.4 compatibility flag.) 839 SB
225 1410 1863 (The value dn is the drive number being defined with this DISKDEF macro invocation. The fsc) 1863 SB
225 1470 1939 (parameter accounts for differing sector numbering systems and is usually 0 to 1. The lsc is the last) 1939 SB
225 1529 1830 (numbered sector on a track. When present, the skf parameter defines the sector skew factor,) 1830 SB
225 1588 1415 (which is used to create a sector translation table according to the skew.) 1415 SB
225 1706 1919 (If the number of sectors is less than 256, a single-byte table is created, otherwise each translation) 1919 SB
225 1765 1949 (table element occupies two bytes. No translation table is created if the skf parameter is omitted, or) 1949 SB
225 1825 211 (equal to 0.) 211 SB
225 1943 1861 (The bls parameter specifies the number of bytes allocated to each data block, and takes on the) 1861 SB
225 2002 1830 (values 1024, 2048, 4096, 8192, or 16384. Generally, performance increases with larger data) 1830 SB
225 2061 1927 (block sizes because there are fewer directory references, and logically connected data records are) 1927 SB
225 2120 1707 (physically close on the disk. Further, each directory entry addresses more data and the) 1707 SB
225 2180 763 (BIOS-resident RAM space is reduced.) 763 SB
225 2298 1867 (The dks parameter specifies the total disk size in bls units.  That is, if the bls = 2048 and dks =) 1867 SB
225 2357 1754 (1000, the total disk capacity is 2,048,000 bytes. If dks is greater than 255, the block size) 1754 SB
225 2416 1914 (parameter bls must be greater than 1024. The value of dir is the total number of directory entries) 1914 SB
225 2475 661 (that might exceed 255, if desired.) 661 SB
225 92 703 (6.11 The DISKDEF Macro Library) 703 SB
