225 227 1867 (Following the ENDEF macro call, a number of uninitialized data areas are defined. These data) 1867 SB
225 286 1938 (areas need not be a part of the BIOS that is loaded upon cold start, but must be available between) 1938 SB
225 345 1905 (the BIOS and the end of memory. The size of the uninitialized RAM area is determined by EQU) 1905 SB
225 405 1934 (statements generated by the ENDEF macro. For a standard four-drive system, the ENDEF macro) 1934 SB
225 464 897 (might produce the following EQU statement:) 897 SB
375 582 201 (4C72 =    ) 201 SB
675 582 602 (BEGDAT EQU $ \(data areas\)) 602 SB
375 641 223 (4DBO =    ) 223 SB
675 641 359 (ENDDAT EQU $) 359 SB
375 700 201 (013C =    ) 201 SB
675 700 550 (DATSIZ EQU $-BEGDAT) 550 SB
225 819 1947 (which indicates that uninitialized RAM begins at location 4C72H, ends at 4DB0H-1, and occupies) 1947 SB
225 878 1872 (013CH bytes. You must ensure that these addresses are free for use after the system is loaded.) 1872 SB
225 996 1929 (After modification, you can use the STAT program to check drive characteristics, because STAT) 1929 SB
225 1055 1881 (uses the disk parameter block to decode the drive information. A STAT command of the form:) 1881 SB
375 1174 301 (STAT D:DSK:) 301 SB
225 1292 1819 (decodes the disk parameter block for drive d \(d = A,...,P\) and displays the following values:) 1819 SB
225 1410 538 (r: 128-byte record capacity) 538 SB
225 1470 500 (k: kilobyte drive capacity) 500 SB
225 1529 539 (d: 32-byte directory entries) 539 SB
225 1588 547 (c: checked directory entries) 547 SB
225 1647 333 (e: records/extent) 333 SB
225 1706 322 (b: records/block) 322 SB
225 1765 301 (s: sectors/track) 301 SB
225 1825 343 (t: reserved tracks) 343 SB
