225 937 784 (6.12 Sector Blocking and Deblocking) 784 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c322 font
225 1055 1887 (Upon each call to BIOS WRITE entry point, the CP/M BDOS includes information that allows) 1887 SB
225 1115 1918 (effective sector blocking and deblocking where the host disk subsystem has a sector size that is a) 1918 SB
225 1174 1877 (multiple of the basic 128-byte unit. The purpose here is to present a general-purpose algorithm) 1877 SB
225 1233 1767 (that can be included within the BIOS and that uses the BDOS information to perform the) 1767 SB
225 1292 499 (operations automatically.) 499 SB
225 1410 1674 (On each call to WRITE, the BDOS provides the following information in register C:) 1674 SB
225 1470 13 ( ) 13 SB
225 1529 565 (     0 = \(normal sector write\)) 565 SB
225 1588 660 (     1 = \(write to directory sector\)) 660 SB
225 1647 1036 (     2 = \(write to the first sector of a new data block\)) 1036 SB
225 1765 1914 (Condition 0 occurs whenever the next write operation is into a previously written area, such as a) 1914 SB
225 1825 1849 (random mode record update; when the write is to other than the first sector of an unallocated) 1849 SB
225 1884 1898 (block; or when the write is not into the directory area. Condition 1 occurs when a write into the) 1898 SB
225 1943 1911 (directory area is performed. Condition 2 occurs when the first record \(only\) of a newly allocated) 1911 SB
225 2002 1927 (data block is written. In most cases, application programs read or write multiple 128-byte sectors) 1927 SB
225 2061 1749 (in sequence; thus, there is little overhead involved in either operation when blocking and) 1749 SB
225 2120 1700 (deblocking records, because preread operations can be avoided when writing records.) 1700 SB
225 92 703 (6.11 The DISKDEF Macro Library) 703 SB
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c322 font
1526 92 649 (CP/M Operating System Manual) 649 SB
1154 2942 92 (6-39) 92 SB
EJ RS
