SS
0 0 25 25 798 1100 300 SM
32 0 0 50 50 0 0 1 45 /MSTT31c322 font
0 0 0 fC
225 227 1900 (Appendix G lists the blocking and deblocking algorithms in skeletal form; this file is included on) 1900 SB
225 286 1872 (your CP/M disk.  Generally, the algorithms map all CP/M sector read operations onto the host) 1872 SB
225 345 1784 (disk through an intermediate buffer that is the size of the host disk sector. Throughout the) 1784 SB
225 405 1839 (program, values and variables that relate to the CP/M sector involved in a seek operation are) 1839 SB
225 464 1794 (prefixed by sek, while those related to the host disk system are prefixed by hst. The equate) 1794 SB
225 523 1887 (statements beginning on line 29 of Appendix G define the mapping between CP/M and the host) 1887 SB
225 582 1540 (system, and must be changed if other than the sample host system is involved.) 1540 SB
225 700 1841 (The entry points BOOT and WBOOT must contain the initialization code starting on line 57,) 1841 SB
225 760 1835 (while the SELDSK entry point must be augmented by the code starting on line 65. Note that) 1835 SB
225 819 1883 (although the SELDSK entry point computes and returns the Disk Parameter Header address, it) 1883 SB
225 878 1738 (does not physically select the host disk at this point \(it is selected later at READHST or) 1738 SB
225 937 1929 (WRITEHST\).  Further, SETTRK and SETMA simply store the values, but do not take any other) 1929 SB
225 996 1779 (action at this point. SECTRAN performs a trivial function of returning the physical sector) 1779 SB
225 1055 162 (number.) 162 SB
225 1174 1860 (The principal entry points are READ and WRITE, starting on lines 110 and 125, respectively.) 1860 SB
225 1233 1624 (These subroutines take the place of your previous READ and WRITE operations.) 1624 SB
225 1351 1828 (The actual physical read or write takes place at either WRITEHST or READHST, where all) 1828 SB
225 1410 1876 (values have been prepared: hstdsk is the host disk number, hsttrk is the host track number, and) 1876 SB
225 1470 1880 (hstsec is the host sector number, which may require translation to physical sector number. You) 1880 SB
225 1529 1943 (must insert code at this point that performs the full sector read or write into or out of the buffer at) 1943 SB
225 1588 1677 (hstbuf of length hstsiz. All other mapping functions are performed by the algorithms.) 1677 SB
225 1706 1820 (This particular algorithm was tested using an 80-megabyte hard disk unit that was originally) 1820 SB
225 1765 1866 (configured for 128-byte sectors, producing approximately 35 megabytes of formatted storage.) 1866 SB
225 1825 1846 (When configured for 512-byte host sectors, usable storage increased to 57 megabytes, with a) 1846 SB
225 1884 1823 (corresponding 400% improvement in overall response. In this situation, there is no apparent) 1823 SB
225 1943 1847 (overhead involved in deblocking sectors, with the advantage that user programs still maintain) 1847 SB
225 2002 1828 (128-byte sectors. This is primarily because of the information provided by the BDOS, which) 1828 SB
225 2061 930 (eliminates the necessity for preread operations.) 930 SB
1033 2239 334 (End of Section 6) 334 SB
