225 882 1920 (produced under a CP/M 1.4 system, the extent mask must be zero and the block size 1K.  This is) 1920 SB
225 941 1878 (because under CP/M 2 an FCB may contain more than one extent.  The number of extents that) 1878 SB
225 1000 1888 (may be contained by an FCB is EXM + 1. The issue is of CP/M 1.4 compatibility also concerns) 1888 SB
225 1060 1941 (random file I/O. To perform random file I/O under CP/M 1.4, you must maintain an FCB for each) 1941 SB
225 1119 1891 (extent of the file.  This scheme is upward compatible with CP/M 2 for files not exceeding 512K) 1891 SB
225 1178 1880 (bytes, the largest file size supported under CP/M 1.4. If you wish to implement random I/O for) 1880 SB
225 1237 1829 (files larger than 512K bytes under CP/M 2, you must use the random read and random write) 1829 SB
225 1296 1891 (functions, BDOS functions 33, 34, and 36.  In this case, only one FCB is used, and if CP/M 1.4) 1891 SB
225 1355 1785 (compatiblity is required, the program must use the return version number function, BDOS) 1785 SB
