225 1121 1789 (sector of the directory. The BDOS always requests a logical sector number between 0 and) 1789 SB
225 1180 1932 (\(SPT-1\). This is typically translated into a physical sector by the BIOS entry point SECTRAN. In) 1932 SB
225 1239 1908 (some disk subsystems, the disk sector size is larger than 128 bytes, usually a power of two, such) 1908 SB
225 1298 1843 (as 256, 512, 1024, or 2048 bytes. These disk sectors are always referred to as host sectors in) 1843 SB
225 1358 1932 (CP/M documentation and should not be confused with other references to sectors, in which cases) 1932 SB
225 1417 1942 (the CP/M 128-byte sectors should be assumed. When the host sector size is larger than 128 bytes,) 1942 SB
225 1476 1860 (host sectors must be buffered in memory and the 128-byte CP/M sectors must be blocked and) 1860 SB
225 1535 1771 (deblocked from them. This can be done by adding an additional module, the blocking and) 1771 SB
225 1594 1635 (deblocking algorithm, between the BIOS disk I/O routines and the actual disk I/O.) 1635 SB
