225 227 1943 (block is given by BLS/128. Physical sectors on the disk media are also numbered consecutively. If) 1943 SB
225 286 1848 (the physical sector size is also 128 bytes, a one-to-one relationship exists between logical and) 1848 SB
225 345 1840 (physical sectors. The logical-to-physical translation table \(XLT\) maps this relationship, and a) 1840 SB
225 405 1891 (skew factor is typically used in generating the table entries. For instance, if the skew factor is 6,) 1891 SB
225 464 255 (XLT will be:) 255 SB
225 582 752 (     Logical:   0  1  2  3  4  5  6 ...... 25) 752 SB
225 641 800 (     Physical:  1  7 13 19 25  5 11 ...... 22) 800 SB
225 760 1913 (The skew factor allows time for program processing without missing the next sector. Otherwise,) 1913 SB
225 819 1948 (the system must wait for an entire disk revolution before reading the next logical sector. The skew) 1948 SB
225 878 1867 (factor can be varied, depending on hardware speed and application processing overhead. Note) 1867 SB
225 937 1871 (that no sector translation is done when the physical sectors are larger than 128 bytes, as sector) 1871 SB
225 996 1261 (deblocking is done in this case. See also sector, SKF, and XLT.) 1261 SB
